---
title: "Loading data"
description: "Readers, formats, and what Tarn infers when you do not tell it."
---

### From a CSV file {#sec-load-csv}

`read_csv` is the reader you will use most. It takes a path and returns a lazy
`Frame` — nothing is read from disk until the query is collected, so pointing it at a
large file costs nothing on its own.

```python
import tarn

sales = tarn.read_csv("sales.csv")
```

Types are inferred from the first block of rows. An all-digit column becomes an
integer, a column with a decimal point becomes a float, anything ISO-8601-shaped
becomes a date, and everything else stays a string. Pass `types=` to override the
guess for named columns when the inference is wrong:

```python
sales = tarn.read_csv("sales.csv", types={"zip": "str"})
```

That override matters more often than it looks. A postal code column of `01450`
infers as an integer and loses its leading zero, and there is no way to recover it
afterwards.

### From Parquet {#sec-load-parquet}

`read_parquet` carries its schema in the file, so nothing is inferred and nothing can
be mis-guessed. It is the format to prefer when you control how the data is written.

```python
sales = tarn.read_parquet("sales.parquet")
```

Column pruning happens at the reader: if the query only ever mentions three columns,
only those three are read off disk. This is the single largest speed difference
between the two readers on a wide table.

### From memory {#sec-load-memory}

A dict of lists becomes a frame directly, which is how most of the examples in this
book are written:

```python
f = tarn.frame({"region": ["north", "south"], "revenue": [10, 20]})
```

This reader is eager — the data is already in memory, so there is nothing to defer.
